<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/14th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">St. George's Night Uprising</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1343">1343</a>–<a href="/years/1345">1345</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as St George's Night Uprising</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">St. George's Night Uprising was a rebellion of the Estonian peasantry in Harju and other districts against their German and Danish lords, begun on the night of 23 April 1343. The rebels killed many Germans and besieged Tallinn but were crushed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a>, after which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> sold its Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> to the Order in 1346.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the Livonian Order conquered Ösel (Saaremaa) in 1261, Northern Crusaders from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> held the whole of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a>. The new rulers imposed taxes and duties, built manor-houses across the country and suppressed native religious practice, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> added its own economic demands. Danish power in Harria and Vironia had weakened by the 14th century, and the province was split between a pro-Danish party led by Bishop Olaf of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roskilde">Roskilde</a> and a pro-German party led by Marquard Breide. Of the Danish vassals in the Duchy of Estonia, 80 per cent were Germans from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a>, 18 per cent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> and 2 per cent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonians">Estonians</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On Saint George's Night, 23 April 1343, a house set alight on a hilltop gave the signal for an attack on the foreigners in Harria. According to the Younger Livonian Rhymed Chronicle, the rebels renounced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, burned the manors and killed the Germans they caught, among them 28 monks of Padise Abbey. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonians">Estonians</a> elected four kings and besieged Reval (Tallinn) with 10,000 men. The uprising spread to Rotalia, where the rebels besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haapsalu">Hapsal</a>.</p><p>The Livonian Master Burchard von Dreileben invited the four kings to talks at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paide">Weissenstein</a> on 4 May and then had them and their squires killed. The chronicle blames the envoys, but some historians see a ruse to kill the leaders. The Order won the Battle of Kanavere on 11 May, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonians">Estonians</a> lost 1,600 men, and the Battle of Sõjamäe on 14 May, where 3,000 were killed. Swedish forces arrived on 18 May, found Reval held by the Order and went home after looting. Reinforcements from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> crushed the remaining resistance in Harria.</p><p>On Ösel the islanders rose on 24 July 1344 and took the Order's castle at Pöide, then killed its defenders after promising them free passage. The rebellion there ended in 1345 with a conditional surrender.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Weakened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narva">Narva</a> in 1345, and in 1346 Valdemar IV sold the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> for 19,000 marks. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">Sovereignty</a> passed on 1 November 1346. The remnants of the pre-Christian Estonian nobility in northern Estonia disappeared. Historians still debate the uprising. Some, such as Edgar V. Saks and Uku Masing, argue from contemporary documents that it was not a fight against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> but against the Livonian Order. Others dismiss this as biased.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Saint George's Night Uprising” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Reval's fortifications proved their worth in 1343 during the <b>St George's Night Uprising</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/reval">Reval · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1343 the Estonians of Harjumaa rose in the <b>St George's Night Uprising</b>, killing many Germans.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-estonia-1219-1346">Duchy of Estonia (1219-1346)</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1343 its peasants began the <b>St George's Night Uprising</b>, which was crushed by the Livonian Order, after which Denmark sold the duchy to the Teutonic Order.</blockquote><a href="/state/harjumaa">Harjumaa</a></li></ul></section>
